    67→\subsection{Hybrid CNN-Transformer Architecture}
    68→\label{sec:cross_attention}
    69→
    70→The hybrid architecture synergistically combines convolutional neural networks for local feature extraction with vision transformers for global context modeling, specifically designed for multi-modal satellite data fusion. The architecture comprises four interconnected components: dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific processing, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, spatial transformers for landscape-scale dependency modeling, and feature pyramid networks for multi-scale representation.
    71→
    72→\textbf{Dual-Path CNN Backbones.} Separate CNN pathways process Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data through structurally similar but parameter-independent networks, acknowledging fundamental differences between SAR backscatter and optical reflectance. Each pathway follows a ResNet-inspired architecture with four progressive stages featuring channel dimensions [64, 128, 256, 512] and spatial downsampling factors [4, 8, 16, 32]. Convolutional blocks incorporate group normalization (32 groups) to handle varying batch sizes, GELU activation for smooth gradients preserving subtle snow boundaries, and modality-specific attention mechanisms. Sentinel-1 features employ Convolutional Block Attention Modules (CBAM) to enhance both channel and spatial attention for SAR texture patterns, while Sentinel-2 features utilize Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) to emphasize spectral relationships critical for optical snow detection \cite{woo2018cbam, wang2020eca}.
    73→
    74→\begin{figure}[!h]
    75→    \centering
    76→    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cnn_backbone_arch.png}
    77→    \caption{Dual-path CNN backbone architecture showing separate processing pathways for Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data, with modality-specific attention mechanisms and progressive feature extraction through four hierarchical stages.}
    78→    \label{fig:cnn_backbone}
    79→\end{figure}
    80→
    81→\textbf{Cross-Attention Fusion Mechanism.} Multi-modal fusion employs bidirectional cross-attention to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies. \added{Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric---SAR provides structural/textural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.}  
    82→
    83→For features $\mathbf{F}_{S1} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_1 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-1 and $\mathbf{F}_{S2} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_2 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-2, the mechanism computes query-key-value attention in both directions:
    84→
    85→\begin{equation}
    86→\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)\mathbf{V}
    87→\end{equation}
    88→
    89→\begin{equation}
    90→\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S1}, \mathbf{K}_{S2}, \mathbf{V}_{S2}), \quad \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S2}, \mathbf{K}_{S1}, \mathbf{V}_{S1})
    91→\end{equation}
    92→
    93→where queries, keys, and values are derived through learned linear projections. The fused representation combines both directional flows: $\mathbf{F}_{fused} = \text{LayerNorm}(\text{Linear}([\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2}; \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1}]))$. The cross-attention employs 8 attention heads with dimension $d_k = 64$, enabling the model to capture different aspects of cross-modal relationships simultaneously, texture correlations, intensity relationships, and spatial pattern correspondences between SAR and optical signatures.
    94→
    95→\begin{figure}[!h]
    96→      \centering
    97→
    98→  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cross_attention_fusion.png}
    99→      \caption{Cross-attention fusion mechanism showing bidirectional query-key-value attention between Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 features. The mechanism computes S1→S2 and S2→S1 attention maps using 8 parallel heads with dimension $d_k=64$, enabling each modality to query relevant information from the other for intelligent multi-modal integration.}
   100→      \label{fig:cross_attention_fusion}
   101→  \end{figure}
   102→
   103→\textbf{Spatial Transformer.} The transformer component addresses convolutional limitations in capturing long-range spatial dependencies essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns influenced by topography and regional climate. The spatial self-attention mechanism preserves 2D topology through learnable relative position encoding:
   104→
   105→\begin{equation}
   106→\text{SpatialAttention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V})_{i,j} = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{q}_i \mathbf{k}_j^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{B}_{i,j}\right)\mathbf{v}_j
   107→\end{equation}
   108→
   109→where $\mathbf{B}_{i,j} = \mathbf{W}_B[\text{rel\_pos}(i, j)]$ represents learnable relative position bias capturing spatial relationships between pixels. This formulation enables the model to learn that nearby pixels have stronger influence while allowing long-range interactions when topographically or climatically relevant. The transformer architecture employs 6 sequential blocks with 8 attention heads and 512 embedding dimensions, providing sufficient capacity for complex spatial relationships while operating on compact feature representations (H/32$\times$W/32) to maintain computational efficiency.
   110→
   111→\textbf{Feature Pyramid Network.} Multi-scale processing through FPN addresses snow cover's inherent scale variability, from pixel-level presence to landscape-level distribution trends. The FPN incorporates adaptive scale selection through attention-weighted fusion:
   112→
   113→\begin{equation}
   114→\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l)} = \mathbf{A}^{(l)} \odot \mathbf{L}^{(l)} + (1 - \mathbf{A}^{(l)}) \odot \text{Upsample}(\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l+1)})
   115→\end{equation}
   116→
   117→where $\mathbf{A}^{(l)} = \sigma(\text{Conv}_{1 \times 1}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{L}^{(l)})))$ represents learned attention weights determining optimal spatial scale for different scenarios. The FPN processes features at four pyramid levels (P2, P3, P4, P5) with 256-channel feature maps, progressively incorporating finer-scale information through bilinear upsampling and attention-weighted fusion from coarsest (P5) to finest (P2) levels.
